# Convergence orders of trapezoid, Simpson and 4-node Gauss–Legendre quadrature

**Question.** How fast do three composite rules converge on three integrands on [0, 1], and does the observed order match theory (2, 4, 8)? **Setup.** Integrands exp(x), 1/(1+25x²), √x with exact integrals e−1, atan(5)/5, 2/3. Rules: composite trapezoid on n panels; composite Simpson (one Simpson rule per panel); composite Gauss–Legendre with 4 nodes per panel (`numpy.polynomial.legendre.leggauss`). n = 2^k, k = 1…12, float64, absolute error. **Fit.** Least squares of ln(error) on ln(n) over points with error > 10⁻¹⁴ (≈45 ε; smaller errors are round-off noise); order = −slope, 95% interval = slope ± t₀.₉₇₅(m−2)·SE, m = points used. Data, code and fit masks: `experiment-repo` `results/errors.csv`, `results/orders.csv`, `code/run.py` (commit 6b32d84). Because the errors are deterministic, the interval measures departure from a straight line, not sampling noise, and says nothing about agreement with the integer theory.

## Fitted orders (float64, cutoff 10⁻¹⁴; order [95% interval], m points)

| function | trapezoid (theory 2) | Simpson (theory 4) | Gauss-4 (theory 8) |
|---|---|---|---|
| exp(x) | 1.9997 [1.9994, 2.0000], 12 | 3.9986 [3.9977, 3.9996], 8 | 7.996, 2 points — no interval |
| 1/(1+25x²) | 2.289 [2.024, 2.554], 12 | 5.665 [4.692, 6.637], 8 | 8.76 [2.93, 14.60], 4 |
| √x | 1.4833 [1.4772, 1.4894], 12 | 1.5000 [1.4999, 1.5000], 12 | 1.5000 [1.5000, 1.5000], 12 |

Moving the cutoff to 10⁻¹³ or 10⁻¹⁵ changes only Simpson on exp (3.9992 at 10⁻¹⁵) and on 1/(1+25x²) (5.39 to 6.01); the other entries do not move (`orders.csv`).

