## Where and why the observed order departs from 2, 4, 8

- **exp(x): theory holds; the float64 data cannot show it for Gauss.** Trapezoid and Simpson reproduce 2 and 4 (Simpson's 3.9986 is pulled down by the first octave, local slope 3.99; it is 4.0000 for k ≥ 5 in the diagnostic below). Gauss-4 reaches round-off (error 0 to ~2·10⁻¹⁶) from k = 3, so only k = 1, 2 are fitted: 7.996 is a one-octave slope with no interval. This is saturation, not a failure of the rule.
- **1/(1+25x²): the fits over k = 1…12 exceed theory and are not asymptotic orders.** The function is analytic but has poles at ±i/5, so the asymptotic regime begins only when the panel width 1/n is well below ≈0.2. For small n the error contains cancellations, giving local slopes far above theory (Simpson up to 11, Gauss up to 21). Hence 2.29, 5.7 and 8.8 (the trapezoid and Simpson intervals exclude 2 and 4; the straight-line model fits poorly, r² ≈ 0.95–0.97, so these intervals are indicative only). Gauss-4 hits the floor from k = 5.
- **√x: all three rules converge at order 1.5** (next section).

**Diagnostic (not part of the float64 experiment).** The same rules in 60-digit arithmetic (`results/orders_highprec.csv`) remove the round-off floor. For k ≥ 5 the orders are exp: 2.0000, 4.0000, 8.0000; 1/(1+25x²): 2.0000, 3.9999, 8.0003 [7.9997, 8.0009]; over all k = 1…12 exp(x) Gauss-4 is 7.9994 [7.9988, 8.0000]. So the theoretical orders 2, 4, 8 are attained for the smooth integrands once n is past the pre-asymptotic range and above the float64 floor.

## √x

√x is not C¹ at 0 (unbounded derivative), so the smoothness assumed by the Euler–Maclaurin and Gauss error expansions fails. Only the panel touching 0 matters: ∫₀^h √x dx scales as h^{3/2}, so the error is ∝ n^{−3/2} for every rule, whatever its nominal order. Simpson and Gauss-4 give 1.5000 [1.4999, 1.5000]; the trapezoid fit, 1.4833 [1.4772, 1.4894], excludes 1.5 only through a pre-asymptotic drift from the next term (h^{5/2}): it is 1.4936 for k ≥ 5 and 1.4966 for k ≥ 8 (diagnostic run). No round-off floor is reached (smallest error ~10⁻⁷–10⁻⁸), so all 12 points are used. Higher order buys only a smaller constant, not a faster rate: at n = 4096 Gauss-4 is far more accurate than the trapezoid rule (7.9·10⁻⁷), at the same rate. A graded mesh or the substitution x = t² would restore the nominal order (suggested, not run).

## Conclusion and limits

Observed orders match theory for exp(x) with trapezoid and Simpson, and for Gauss-4 once round-off is removed; they exceed theory in the whole-range fits for 1/(1+25x²) because of the pole-limited pre-asymptotic regime; they are 1.5 for √x for all rules. The cutoff 10⁻¹⁴ is a choice, and fits with 2–4 points (Gauss-4 on exp and on 1/(1+25x²)) carry little or no information. The cover image is decorative and carries no result.
